{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/aa927160974aa22e09f1a8c0b47d588a63f22fc2dd018c3bba36d4a00c649435.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-126610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/ae26451a8f2962ed873fc5eb703197375bc1a02eff2240bd21cebac81ef26205.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})=1-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(1-y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(1-2y+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1-y)}^{2}\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/2baa8f39370b831bd8306aa3eb60e5d16d54ce50474f873f0fa329c069d61689.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=e,$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/12d30907e2fb6cdd031134a667c7de1324fec66025c0f2029b18f4521e94da83.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=3+2y-{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y-3 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3+2y-{y}^{2})=2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}-8y+4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(1-y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{4}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/3d01ddb2278875780397e90a9d6f6ad6b2fc44890c59faab7a69b8ebcb5d210b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=4-(y-4)^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-(y-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=6.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4-(y-4)^{2})=8-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(4-(y-4)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(8-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-2y+5}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/bbdb10a20dd90cd0928e91851662fccaf8d0b03e86c44f58b5311ba805183e8e.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x+3$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4x+3)=2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/f70f186e51a312b26dc4e2cfa3a4d812fab6583ada8b8af82f153a9975948b4b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)=2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-3x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/21d8b516e09e592bd77c815ba50b10faed648386013f9d0a618df5ec758b9a91.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=5x-{x}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(5-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-{x}^{3})=5-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{1+{(5-3{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{12-5{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-353019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=1+{x}^{2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-353019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+{x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+4{x}^{2}.$ Then, $8x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-353019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-353019" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-353019" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y}{2\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi \\,y{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "8\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-104529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y={(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}(-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/3}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}={x}^{-2/3}(4-{x}^{2/3})=4{x}^{-2/3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{-2/3}-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{-2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "4\\pi \\sqrt[3]{2}"] ["math" "5\\pi \\sqrt[3]{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{20\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt[4]{3}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{y}^{4}.$ Then, ${y}^{3}\\text{d}y=(\\frac{1}{4})\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{8\\pi }{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-126731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})={x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=x.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{2(4)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-(\\frac{2(1)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{116}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{232\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "df16f6a99800b716ced898b77e8d0315baf0562d6cc36836bf79f389521adec4"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{116\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{13\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{201\\pi }{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{232\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\frac{81\\pi }{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "q-109413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{232\\pi }{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/state.json" "file_sha256" "f18683ebb3d152df488cba30a359b76ba03f6741f1913300fcbe227816dd2643"} {"question" "e-8907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/30c2b0000055802166e5bdf8ec3e305d83e65ac960043f66463702952d91e4d6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-8907.json" "file_sha256" "eb88bcd451d9ac1723fc41146f0559780eee55484344c0db0921079190ed0b04"} {"question" "e-8907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-8907.json" "file_sha256" "eb88bcd451d9ac1723fc41146f0559780eee55484344c0db0921079190ed0b04"} {"question" "e-9121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/777c36689da6f6cbcf4ce92a7c5ba17c000a7c442a972851d9d1cafaa9fb2ba4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-9121.json" "file_sha256" "3f5b89398fae947121ddacc7763280f4095f6b022288212dc17a94ed446638c2"} {"question" "e-9121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x= \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-3x)=3{x}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+(3{x}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}-18{x}^{2}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-9121.json" "file_sha256" "3f5b89398fae947121ddacc7763280f4095f6b022288212dc17a94ed446638c2"} {"question" "e-8906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-8906.json" "file_sha256" "ce90e4726553e0a171856c1838ad50911c964d6110599d6b58345df8766414b2"} {"question" "e-8906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/example-8906.json" "file_sha256" "ce90e4726553e0a171856c1838ad50911c964d6110599d6b58345df8766414b2"}] "usage" {"e-8907" [] "q-109253" [] "q-104529" [] "e-9121" [] "q-104518" [] "e-8906" [] "q-109413" []} "basis" 2861 "retractions" [[17592186176155 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3632/Source/Images/q-109253.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x = 3 + 2y - y^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?"] [17592186176130 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y = \\sin x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = \\frac{π}{2}$. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?"] [17592186176131 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y = 0$ and $y = \\sqrt{2}$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis."] [17592186176160 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y = \\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 3$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis."] [17592186175750 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2$, as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3632/Source/Images/99e6227dbd52eb759c011421688d7d6f.png)"] [17592186175750 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{2}) = 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{y_{1}}^{y_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dx}{dy})^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}(y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + (2y)^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}y^{2}\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186175765 :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3632/Source/Images/da72f86944875b96d066b7c3305ca7e0.png)"] [17592186175765 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 3x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3} - 3x) = 3x^{2} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1 + (3x^{2} - 3)^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9x^{4} - 18x^{2} + 10}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186175749 :question/problem "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis."] [17592186175749 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1 + 4}dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}∫_{0}^{2}xdx \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}[\\frac{2^{2}}{2} - \\frac{0^{2}}{2}] \\\\\n&= 4π\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173443 17592186173444 17592186173445 17592186173446 17592186173447 17592186173449 17592186173450 17592186173451 17592186173452 17592186173453 17592186173593 17592186173594 17592186173595 17592186173596 17592186173597 17592186173623 17592186173624 17592186173625 17592186173626 17592186173627] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173442 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173442 :answer-field/id] [17592186173442 :answer-field/key] [17592186173442 :answer-field/type] [17592186173448 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173448 :answer-field/id] [17592186173448 :answer-field/key] [17592186173448 :answer-field/type] [17592186173592 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173592 :answer-field/id] [17592186173592 :answer-field/key] [17592186173592 :answer-field/type] [17592186173622 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173622 :answer-field/id] [17592186173622 :answer-field/key] [17592186173622 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []